In article <CEDMAN.90Nov25103718@lynx.ps.uci.edu>, cedman@golem.ps.uci.edu (Carl
Edman) says:
>
>Attunement allows the following little unrealistic trick:
>
>e.g. Medium P. Owerful, the 15th level player with moderate means,
>wants to overcome the castle level. But he really isn't powerful
>enough to have any chance of taking out all the monsters in the
>courtroom. So what does he do ?
>
>He ask two or three of his friends to create 1st levelers (quickly
>brought up to 5th level, e.g.) and come with him to the castle. He
>sends them into the room first and most monsters attune to them. But
>they do not fight. They simply do nothing. So most monsters don't do
>anything either. So they don't get attacked.    <<etc.>>

Sure the monsters would do something!  Many monsters start out always
hostile, so they will start banging on your little guys right away.
Once attuned, they are free to attack at will.  If no-one bothers to
attack these monsters they will just bang on the nearest player target.
When Medium walks in and attacks, the nearest beasties will beat on
him, and if possible, follow him out of the room.  This allows the
second/third rank of monsters to move forward and attack your "innocent"
low-level characters.  My understanding of "attunement" is that a
monster will only attack one target at a time and that it takes a turn
to switch targets -- nothing about total inactivity.

It may be that your low-level friends will just sit tight and not use
their keyboards.  This may prevent any monster from hitting your friends
twice, but your low-levels may receive a lot of first-strikes.
>
>Another example (Beware: I haven't fought the wizard, so this may be
>incorrect , but it applies equally well to any high-level monster):
>
>2 high-level players arrive at the Wizard's. Wizard attunes to one of
>them. That one stops. In the meantime the other players walks to the
>other end of the room and brings himself into perfect position. The
>second player fires an arrow(or spell) on the Wizard. The Wizard
>attunes to the second one. The first player gets into perfect position
>and does a long range-attack on the wizard. And then the second player
>again. a.s.o. Poor old wizzy hasn't got a chance.
>
As I remember, changing attunement is not necessarily automatic.  If
your high-level monster is busy mauling the player right in front of
him, he may ignore incoming arrow from the opposite end of the room.
If your worried that Mister Monster won't do anything because maulee
is inactive, perhaps "attunement" can be defined as follows: monster
may attune to last to hit him, but still chooses most sensible target.

   Black Dragon mauls Fred.
   Bill shoots from across the room.
   Black Dragon attunes to Bill and mauls Fred.
   Fred hits Black Dragon.
   Black Dragon attunes to Fred, but doesn't get to maul him.
   Bill shoots across from room.
   Black Dragon attunes to Bill and mauls Fred.  (etc)

